      "id": "9b113cea-2b3b-4808-a9be-14ea09a36c07",
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "#### Font changing task\n\nThe default font in Java graphics is boring. You can change it to essentially any font on your computer. If you have a Mac, the FontBook app will show you all those fonts. On Windows, you can find them in the Settings app.\n\nA font has three components.\n\n* The family, like \"Arial\", \"Times New Roman\", or \"Helvetica\"\n* The weight/posture: bold, italic or both.\n* The size, just like the one when you change the font size in Word or Google Docs.\n\nTo create a new `Font` object, we specify all three of these components. For example, \n\n```java\nFont labelFont = new Font(\"Arial\", Font.BOLD, 16);\n```\n\ncreates a new `Font` object with the identifier `labelFont`, family \"Arial\" in bold, and with size 16 points.\n\nIf we want to have an italic font, we would use `Font.ITALIC` for the second argument. If we want to have both bold and italic, we would use `Font.BOLD | Font.ITALIC`. If we just want a plain font, we would use `Font.PLAIN`\n\nThen, we can ask the `colorLabel` object to use that font to display its text by invoking the `setFont` behavior. That would look like\n\n```java\ncolorLabel.setFont(labelFont);\n```\n\nHere's your task for the code cell below.\n\n1. Add the two lines of code in this cell to the code cell below. Run everything to see that the font has changed.\n2. Define a second `Font` object with any choice of family, weight/posture, and size, and apply that to your second label. ",
      "metadata": {
        "language": "markdown",
        "id": "9b113cea-2b3b-4808-a9be-14ea09a36c07"
      }
    },
