    {
      "id": "ebd74446-49ba-40d5-af73-b96416b543f7",
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "#### Optional challenge: Adding an image to a `JLabel` object\n\nYou do not need to complete challenge exercises to get full credit for the project. I recommend these exercises for anyone who wants to extend and practice their programming skills further. These exercises will have less detailed instructions, but enough so that you should be able to work through them if you have a good understanding of what you've done already. Do not attempt challenge exercises until the rest of the project is done. And you can certainly ask questions about these exercises!\n\nSo here's the task description.\n\nThe labels we're using can display images as well as text. Java can display JPEG, PNG, or GIF images (including animated gifs). The image will be in a file in your repository and it must have the extension .jpg, .png, or .gif\n\nOnce you have an image in your repository, you will create an `ImageIcon` object that will be the background for your label instead of the color. \n\nHere's the plan that you should follow. The steps up to the extra challenge require only three lines of code, but getting those three lines exactly right with a correct image file in the correct location is the challenge.\n\n1. Drag an image file to the repository. The image should be pretty small, probably no more than 200x200 pixels.\n2. Tell Java we plan to use the `ImageIcon` functionality by importing javax.swing.ImageIcon. See the magic code for how to do an import.\n3. In the next code cell, create an `ImageIcon` object using a line like\n\n    ```java\n    ImageIcon icon = new ImageIcon(\"/Users/rwhitnel/Documents/GitHub/CTIS210Fall26/projects/CTIS_210_Project_3/avatar.png\");\n    ```\n\n    where the argument is the *full path* to your image file. You may not be familiar with getting that, so ask me if you're not sure. There are a lot of ways to do this wrong, but once it's working, it gives you a lot of power.\n4. Attach the `icon` object to your label by invoking the `setIcon` method.\n5. Extra special challenge: If your image isn't the size you want, research how to modify the size of an image and then follow the instructions above to complete this exercise.",
      "metadata": {
        "language": "markdown",
        "id": "ebd74446-49ba-40d5-af73-b96416b543f7"
      }
    },
